==English==
{{Webster 1913}}

===Etymology===
{{bor|en|la|extortus}}, past participle of {{m|la|extorquere||to [[twist]] or [[wrench]] out, to extort}}; from {{m|la|ex||out}} + {{m|la|-tort}}, from {{m|la|torqueō||twist, turn}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{rhymes|ɔː(r)t|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[take]] or [[seize]] off an [[unwilling]] person by physical force, menace, duress, torture, or any undue or illegal exercise of power or ingenuity
#: ''to '''extort''' contributions from the vanquished''
#: ''to '''extort''' confessions of guilt''
#: ''to '''extort''' a promise''
#: ''to '''extort''' payment of a debt''
# {{lb|en|transitive|legal}} To obtain by means of the offense of [[extortion]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|and|intransitive|medicine|ophthalmology}} To twist outwards.

====Synonyms====
* {{sense|take by force}} [[wrench]] away (from); to [[tear]] away; to [[wring]] (from); to [[exact]]

